This is a simple viewer for PassBook® files.

Open a .pkpass file or .pkpasses bundle from Downloads, a file manager, or
"Open pass file" on the Import page. Confirm the listed passes to copy them
into the app's private data directory, alongside saved cards. The app no
longer searches personal folders for passes. Previously discovered files
need to be opened and imported; the original files are left untouched.
Imported passes remain available after the source download is deleted.
Passes with the same issuer and serial number replace the existing copy
after confirmation. Bundles are validated before any passes are imported.
Imported pass barcodes use the system ZXing writer, preserving the message's
declared byte encoding. Generated QR, Aztec, PDF417 and Code 128 images are
decoded again and checked against those bytes before display. Unsupported or
unrepresentable messages show an error instead of a substituted barcode.
A tap on the pass shows the details including the
barcode (usually the most important thing). The design is simplified compared
to the original app, but should be readable.

Passes close to the relevant time are highlighted. It's also possible to
highlight passes close to relevant coordinates (check settings). The cover displays the barcode of the pass or card currently open in a detail
view. The code is fitted inside a white square, with a small app icon above and the
pass/card icon below. When no code is open, the cover shows the large app icon
and Pass Viewer title.


Saved cards
-----------
Use "Import" in the pull-down menu to scan a barcode or QR code, give it a
name, and save it alongside imported passes. Use "Choose image" below the
viewfinder to read a code from a photo or screenshot instead. Crop images with multiple codes
to the one you want before importing. Tap a saved card to display its
code; tap the code to show it fullscreen. Long-press the list entry to delete it.
Cards stay in the app's private data directory and are available offline.
Scanned cards live in its cards/ directory; imported pass packages in passes/.
Use "Change icon" in a saved card's menu (or long-press its list entry) to
search Iconify's brand and general icon catalogs or choose an image. Preview
the icon, optionally adjust a square crop, then tap "Use icon". Icons are
stored with the card for offline display; catalog source and license details
remain available on the icon page. "Use default icon" removes a custom icon.
The camera uses a square viewfinder: keep the code inside it, pinch to zoom,
and tap the code to focus. Continuous autofocus resumes after five seconds.

Scanning requires camera permission, Qt Multimedia, and ZXing >= 3.0.2 (available
in the Sailfish OS 5.2 SDK). The app remains compatible with Qt 5.6; the ZXing
headers require a C++17 compiler. Common QR, Code 128, Code 39, EAN/UPC, ITF,
Aztec, Data Matrix and PDF417 codes are supported. Each saved image is checked
against the scanned format and bytes. Codes that cannot be reproduced faithfully,
including incomplete multi-symbol codes, are rejected. Rotating or time-limited
codes retain the value scanned and are not refreshed automatically.

The list always uses timeline order: undated cards first, then dated passes newest first, with
current passes in bold. The Archive page holds automatically and manually
archived items. Long-press an entry to archive or restore it. In Settings, the
Time section has an independent auto-archive delay, including Never. Archiving
does not move or delete the original files. Restored items stay in the main list.

See tests/README.md for tests and device validation.

Sharing
-------
Use Share in a pass/card menu or long-press menu to open Sailfish sharing.
Imported passes are shared unchanged, including their issuer signatures.
Scanned cards are exported locally as unsigned .pkpass files with their name,
icon and original barcode image. QR, Aztec, PDF417 and Code 128 also include
standard barcode fields when those can preserve the scanned code's semantics.
Other formats and GS1/ECI variants retain their exact image rather than being
converted to another barcode type. Pass Viewer displays that image in normal,
simple and fullscreen views; other viewers may use the included strip image.
These generated unsigned passes are not intended for Apple Wallet, which
requires passes signed with an Apple-issued certificate.

Sharing uses owner-only files in the application's shared-passes/ cache.
Snapshots remain available after the sharing sheet closes or the app exits,
so delayed transfers do not lose their attachments. They can be removed by
clearing the app cache once transfers have finished. Original passes and saved
cards are not modified. Icon attribution is included in exported passes.
